A slice holds a pointer into a backing array, plus len and cap. When you slice a large array/slice, that pointer still points inside the big array — so even though the small slice only 'sees' a few elements, the whole backing array stays pinned in memory as long as that slice is alive, since the garbage collector can't free something still referenced. That's the leak: actual memory usage is much bigger than len suggests.


s := make([]int, 0)
	for i := 0; i < 10; i++ {
		s = append(s, i)
		fmt.Printf("len=%d cap=%d\n", len(s), cap(s))
	}
